---
description: Strongly connected components and elementary cycles of a directed graph.
import:
  - plain_parser_base
---

***definitions***

- :Graph: is a directed graph given as a mapping from each node to the list of its successor nodes.
  - A node that appears only as a successor is a node with no successors.
  - Node order is the iteration order of the mapping, followed by successor-only nodes in order of first mention.

- :GraphSearch: is the `plain_parser.graph` module of :PythonApi:.

***implementation reqs***

- :GraphSearch: finds components with Tarjan's algorithm and cycles with Johnson's algorithm (D. B. Johnson, SIAM J. Comput. 4(1):77-84, 1975).

- :GraphSearch: keeps its own stack of frames instead of recursing, so the depth of a :Graph: is not bounded by the Python recursion limit.

***functional specs***

# find-strongly-connected-components
:GraphSearch: returns the strongly connected components of a :Graph: as a list of node sets.
- Every node, successor-only nodes included, is in exactly one component.
- A node on no cycle is a component of its own.
- A :Graph: whose longest path is twice the Python recursion limit is handled.

# list-simple-cycles
:GraphSearch: yields every elementary cycle of a :Graph: exactly once, each as the list of its nodes in edge order.
- Each node of a cycle has the next node as a successor, and the last node has the first.
- A node that is its own successor is a one-node cycle; all one-node cycles come first, in node order.
- Every longer cycle starts at its earliest node in node order, and longer cycles come in non-decreasing order of that first node.
- A successor listed twice for one node does not repeat any cycle.
- An empty :Graph: and a :Graph: without cycles yield nothing.

# limit-cycle-search
:GraphSearch: takes an optional cycle limit and stops after yielding that many cycles.
- A limit of 0 yields nothing; no limit yields every cycle.
- Cycles are found lazily: a :Graph: with billions of cycles and a limit of 11 returns 11 cycles at once.
- Nodes that lie on no cycle are never searched, so an acyclic part with exponentially many paths costs nothing.
- A cycle longer than twice the Python recursion limit is found.
